00:00:03.409 --> 00:00:04.789 align:start position:0%
we cross our fingers for luck we've been  doing it for centuries  but why originally the gesture needed  two people it was an ancient pagan  custom for people to cross fingers with  each other they believed their wishes  would be kept safe at the point where  their fingers crossed until they came  true this idea of capturing good fortune  also explains the practice of tying  string around a finger for Celts and  anglo-saxons  it would literally tether good luck to a  person the gesture also has roots within  Christianity when faced with persecution  early Christians would cross their  fingers to invoke the power of Christ's  crucifixion and in a secret handshake  they would cross fingers with fellow  Christians before gathering together in  safety for mass the handshake made the  Christian symbol of the Ichthus or sign  of the fish it's thought that Christians  who were persecuted would cross their  fingers to absolve themselves from the  lies they told to their tormentors and  that is why we cross our fingers behind  our back when making a promise we don't  intend to keep in England in the 16th  century people cross their fingers to  protect themselves from evil coughs and  sneezes in a 1787 book of popular  superstitions it recommended keeping  your fingers crossed  after walking under a ladder until you  see a dog  but it is the gestures association with  good luck that has stood the test of  time a recent University College of  London study found that crossing fingers  confuses the way our brains processed  pain signals actually reducing painful  sensations and if that's true then that  would be a bit of good luck for everyone  [Music]
 

